  "worked_solution": "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12x+y+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ 3(0)^{2}+{y}^{2}-12(0)+y+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+y+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y+\\frac{1}{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-\\frac{1}{2}\\,).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/assets/94a5bd2df6ae6177e62b66a0a978a358d73fed255e425337812887c81b9d2d10.png)",
